<script>
'use strict';

/* ─── Question bank ───────────────────────────────────────── */
const ALL_Q = [

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
// GRADIENT BOOSTING
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

{id:'mc-gbm-residuals', type:'mc', channel:'gbm', ch_label:'Gradient Boosting',
 text:`In gradient boosting with squared-error loss, what does each new tree learn to predict?`,
 opts:[
   {t:'The residuals of the current ensemble — the difference between the target and what the model predicts so far.',c:true,
    w:'Correct. For MSE loss, the negative gradient equals y − F(x) (residuals). Each tree fits a function approximating the current error.'},
   {t:'The original target values, independently re-sampled with replacement from the training data.',c:false,
    w:'That describes Random Forest (bagging). In GBM, each tree corrects the previous ensemble\'s errors — residuals, not re-sampled targets.'},
   {t:'The absolute value of the prediction error, so that large mistakes get larger trees.',c:false,
    w:'Wrong — the gradient of squared error is the signed residual (y − ŷ), not the absolute error. MAE loss uses sign(y − ŷ) as the gradient.'},
   {t:'The raw input features projected onto the direction of maximum variance.',c:false,
    w:'Wrong — that describes PCA dimensionality reduction. GBM trees fit residuals in the target space, not projections of features.'},
 ]},

{id:'mc-gbm-lr', type:'mc', channel:'gbm', ch_label:'Gradient Boosting',
 text:`The model in <code>paper_trading_sim.py:123</code> uses <code>GradientBoostingRegressor(random_state=42)</code> with no other arguments. What are the sklearn defaults for <code>n_estimators</code> and <code>learning_rate</code>?`,
 opts:[
   {t:'n_estimators=100, learning_rate=0.1',c:true,
    w:'Correct. sklearn GradientBoostingRegressor defaults: n_estimators=100, learning_rate=0.1, max_depth=3, subsample=1.0.'},
   {t:'n_estimators=100, learning_rate=0.01',c:false,
    w:'Wrong — learning_rate defaults to 0.1 in sklearn, not 0.01. AdaBoost defaults to 1.0; XGBoost defaults to 0.3. Each library differs.'},
   {t:'n_estimators=200, learning_rate=0.1',c:false,
    w:'Wrong — sklearn\'s default is 100 trees, not 200. The docstring confirms: n_estimators=100 as the baseline.'},
   {t:'n_estimators=50, learning_rate=0.05',c:false,
    w:'Wrong — sklearn\'s defaults are n_estimators=100 and learning_rate=0.1. The repo uses these exact defaults (no hyperparameter tuning).'},
 ]},

{id:'wr-gbm-funcspace', type:'wr', channel:'gbm', ch_label:'Gradient Boosting',
 text:'Explain <strong>function-space gradient descent</strong> as used in gradient boosting. What "gradient" is being descended, and what does adding a new tree correspond to in that framing?',
 concepts:[
   {label:'Loss is a function of F(x), not weights', terms:['loss','function','f(x)','ensemble','prediction','gradient of loss']},
   {label:'Gradient w.r.t. F(x) = residuals for MSE', terms:['residual','gradient','mse','squared','y - f','y−f','y - ŷ']},
   {label:'New tree fits the negative gradient', terms:['new tree','fit','negative gradient','approximate','residual','correct']},
   {label:'Step scaled by learning rate ν', terms:['learning rate','nu','ν','scale','step','contribution','shrink']},
   {label:'Additive update: F += ν × tree', terms:['additive','update','add','f +=','accumulate','ensemble grows']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
Standard gradient descent updates <em>weights</em> θ by θ ← θ − η·∇_θ L. In boosting, the parameters are the prediction function F(x) itself — we take a "gradient step in function space."
<br><br>
At round m, the "gradient" we need is ∂L/∂F(xᵢ) for each training point. For MSE: ∂½(yᵢ − F(xᵢ))²/∂F = −(yᵢ − F(xᵢ)) = −residualᵢ.
<br><br>
We can't subtract an arbitrary function directly, so we fit a <strong>shallow tree</strong> to approximate −gradient = the residuals. That tree is the "direction" of steepest descent in function space.
<br><br>
Then we update: F_{m+1}(x) = F_m(x) + ν × h_m(x), where ν is the learning rate (step size) and h_m is the new tree. The ensemble grows one tree at a time, each step reducing the loss.
<br><br>
<strong>In the repo:</strong> <code>GradientBoostingRegressor(n_estimators=100, learning_rate=0.1)</code> runs 100 such rounds with ν=0.1. The final prediction is F(x) = Σ 0.1 × h_m(x).`
},

{id:'wr-gbm-lr-tradeoff', type:'wr', channel:'gbm', ch_label:'Gradient Boosting',
 text:'Explain the <strong>learning rate ν as a bias-variance tradeoff</strong>. Why does lower ν (e.g. 0.01) + more trees generally generalize better than higher ν (0.5) + fewer trees, given fixed compute?',
 concepts:[
   {label:'Small ν makes each step conservative', terms:['conserv','small step','cautious','slow','smaller contribution']},
   {label:'More trees needed to reach same training loss', terms:['more tree','compensate','same loss','n_estimators','more rounds']},
   {label:'Each tree corrects a smaller fraction of error → less overfitting per tree', terms:['overfit','variance','less memoriz','noise','generali']},
   {label:'Shrinkage regularizes the ensemble', terms:['regulariz','shrinkage','prevent','overfit','shrink','control']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
With large ν: each tree's signal is amplified, so the ensemble reaches low training loss quickly with few trees. But each tree has a chance to fit noise patterns aggressively — the ensemble overfits.
<br><br>
With small ν: each tree contributes only a tiny fraction of its signal. To reach the same training loss you need many more trees, but each individual tree is a weaker "correction" — there's less opportunity for any single tree to memorize training-set noise.
<br><br>
<strong>Shrinkage as regularization:</strong> ν < 1 acts like L2 regularization in the function space. The "ridge" is built in: no single tree can dominate the ensemble because its contribution is scaled down. The combination of many small steps explores the loss surface more carefully.
<br><br>
<strong>Practical tradeoff:</strong> Lower ν requires proportionally more compute (more trees), but generalization improves. Lesson 0025 notes the repo's defaults (ν=0.1, M=100) are untuned — a Bayesian search over ν and M on walk-forward Sharpe is the correct tuning procedure.`
},

{id:'wr-gbm-vs-rf', type:'wr', channel:'gbm', ch_label:'Gradient Boosting',
 text:'Compare <strong>Gradient Boosting</strong> vs <strong>Random Forest</strong> at the algorithmic level. State two key differences. Which tends to have lower bias? Which lower variance?',
 concepts:[
   {label:'Sequential vs parallel (independent) trees', terms:['sequential','parallel','independent','series','order','one at a time']},
   {label:'GBM corrects errors (boosting); RF averages independent models (bagging)', terms:['boosting','bagging','error','average','resample','bootstrap','correction']},
   {label:'GBM lower bias (can reduce to near zero in-sample)', terms:['lower bias','gbm','overfit','flexible','in-sample']},
   {label:'RF lower variance (averaging independent trees reduces variance)', terms:['lower variance','rf','random forest','independent','average','stable']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
<strong>Algorithmic differences:</strong>
<ol style="margin:.4rem 0 .4rem 1.2rem;line-height:1.8">
  <li><strong>Sequential vs parallel:</strong> GBM builds trees one at a time, each fitting the previous ensemble's residuals. RF builds trees independently in parallel (bootstrap samples + random feature subsets), then averages.</li>
  <li><strong>Error correction vs error averaging:</strong> GBM explicitly reduces remaining error each round (boosting). RF reduces variance by averaging uncorrelated estimates (bagging). GBM optimizes a loss; RF averages for stability.</li>
</ol>
<strong>Bias-Variance:</strong> GBM has lower bias (each round explicitly drives training error toward zero — given enough trees and small ν, training loss → 0). RF has lower variance (averaged independent trees cancel out idiosyncratic noise). In practice, GBM overfits more easily, which is why walk-forward OOS evaluation is essential.`
},

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
// OLS & LINEAR REGRESSION
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

{id:'mc-ols-dof', type:'mc', channel:'ols', ch_label:'OLS & Regression',
 text:`In <code>solutions/03_stat_arb_risk/pairs.py:18</code>:
<pre class="snip">dof = <span class="fn">len</span>(y) - X.<span class="hi">shape</span>[<span class="num">1</span>]</pre>
What does <code>dof</code> represent and why is it <code>len(y) − X.shape[1]</code>?`,
 opts:[
   {t:'Degrees of freedom for variance estimation: n observations minus p parameters (including the intercept column in X).',c:true,
    w:'Correct. X.shape[1] = number of columns including the constant. Dividing residual sum of squares by (n − p) gives the unbiased estimator σ̂². This is the denominator of the t-statistic.'},
   {t:'The number of residuals that are free to vary after fitting the regression line through all data points.',c:false,
    w:'This is the colloquial definition and is correct in spirit, but the answer about what dof equals numerically is (a).'},
   {t:'The number of parameters estimated minus one, used as a Bessel\'s correction for finite-sample standard errors.',c:false,
    w:'Wrong — Bessel\'s correction for univariate std is (n-1), but in regression the correction is (n - p) where p is the number of fitted parameters.'},
   {t:'The length of the residual vector minus two, accounting for estimating both slope and intercept separately.',c:false,
    w:'Wrong — X.shape[1] counts all columns (intercept + all slopes), so dof = n − number_of_columns. For simple regression (1 slope + 1 intercept), dof = n − 2; for multiple regression with k slopes + intercept, dof = n − (k+1).'},
 ]},

{id:'mc-ols-lstsq', type:'mc', channel:'ols', ch_label:'OLS & Regression',
 text:`<code>pairs.py:16</code> uses <code>np.linalg.lstsq(X, y, rcond=None)</code> rather than <code>np.linalg.inv(X.T @ X) @ X.T @ y</code>. Why?`,
 opts:[
   {t:'lstsq uses QR/SVD decomposition which is numerically stable when X is near-singular or collinear; direct inversion amplifies rounding errors.',c:true,
    w:'Correct. The normal equations form (X\'X)⁻¹X\'y amplify errors in ill-conditioned X\'X. lstsq uses QR or SVD — stable even when X is rank-deficient or nearly so.'},
   {t:'lstsq is faster by a constant factor for all matrix sizes since it avoids forming X.T @ X explicitly.',c:false,
    w:'Wrong — lstsq is not always faster; it performs more work (QR/SVD) than a naive matrix multiply + invert. The advantage is stability, not speed.'},
   {t:'np.linalg.inv only accepts square matrices, while X.T @ X may not be square when rows < columns.',c:false,
    w:'Wrong — X.T @ X is always square (p × p). The problem with inv is numerical instability when X.T @ X is near-singular, not a shape error.'},
   {t:'lstsq automatically handles missing values in X by dropping rows before solving.',c:false,
    w:'Wrong — lstsq does not handle NaNs. The feature matrix must be pre-cleaned. The advantage of lstsq is numerical stability via QR/SVD.'},
 ]},

{id:'wr-ols-normal-eq', type:'wr', channel:'ols', ch_label:'OLS & Regression',
 text:'Walk through the <strong>normal equations</strong> for OLS. Starting from "minimize the sum of squared residuals," derive the β̂ formula. Why should you use <code>lstsq</code> rather than computing <code>inv(X\'X)</code> directly?',
 concepts:[
   {label:'Objective: minimize ‖y − Xβ‖²', terms:['minimize','sum of squared','residual','‖y','loss']},
   {label:'Take derivative, set to zero: X\'Xβ = X\'y', terms:['derivative','x\'x','normal equation','x.t','xt','first order']},
   {label:'Solution: β̂ = (X\'X)⁻¹X\'y', terms:['β̂','beta hat','inverse','solution','(x\'x)⁻¹']},
   {label:'lstsq uses QR/SVD — stable when X\'X is ill-conditioned', terms:['lstsq','qr','svd','stable','ill-condition','near-singular','collinear']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
<strong>Derivation:</strong>
<ol style="margin:.4rem 0 .4rem 1.2rem;line-height:1.8">
  <li>Objective: minimize L(β) = ‖y − Xβ‖² = (y − Xβ)ᵀ(y − Xβ).</li>
  <li>Expand: L = yᵀy − 2βᵀXᵀy + βᵀXᵀXβ.</li>
  <li>Differentiate w.r.t. β, set to zero: ∂L/∂β = −2Xᵀy + 2XᵀXβ = 0.</li>
  <li>Normal equations: <strong>XᵀXβ = Xᵀy</strong>.</li>
  <li>Solution: <strong>β̂ = (XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀy</strong> (when XᵀX is invertible).</li>
</ol>
<strong>Why lstsq:</strong> Forming XᵀX then calling inv() is numerically unstable when features are collinear (XᵀX near-singular). Tiny eigenvalues in XᵀX become huge after inversion, amplifying rounding error. <code>np.linalg.lstsq</code> uses QR or SVD internally — stable even for rank-deficient X. In trading, features like MA5 and MA10 are highly correlated, making this stability crucial.`
},

{id:'wr-r2', type:'wr', channel:'ols', ch_label:'OLS & Regression',
 text:'Define <strong>R²</strong>. What does it mean for OOS R² to be negative? What should you expect for a well-calibrated daily-return prediction model in walk-forward OOS evaluation?',
 concepts:[
   {label:'R² = 1 − SSR/TSS', terms:['1 - ssr','1 − ssr','tss','total sum','ssr','residual sum']},
   {label:'Fraction of variance explained', terms:['fraction','variance','explained','proportion']},
   {label:'Negative OOS R²: model worse than predicting the mean', terms:['negative','worse than mean','mean prediction','baseline','0','below']},
   {label:'OOS R² 1-5% is typical for daily returns', terms:['1%','2%','3%','4%','5%','small','few percent','daily','normal','typical']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
<strong>R² = 1 − SSR/TSS</strong> where SSR = Σ(yᵢ − ŷᵢ)² (residual sum of squares) and TSS = Σ(yᵢ − ȳ)² (total sum of squares).
<br><br>
R² measures the fraction of variance in y explained by the model. R²=1.0: perfect; R²=0: same as always predicting the mean; R²<0: model is <em>worse</em> than just predicting ȳ.
<br><br>
<strong>Negative OOS R²:</strong> When the model was tuned on a training set with characteristics that don't generalize, it can actively harm predictions on new data. The signal learned is noise-specific.
<br><br>
<strong>Realistic expectation:</strong> For daily equity return prediction with GBM in a walk-forward OOS evaluation, R² of 0.01–0.05 (1–5%) is normal. This is not "basically zero" — a model with consistent OOS R² > 0 outperforms random guessing every day. Combined with cross-sectional selection (top-N tickers), even 2% R² can produce positive alpha. The in-sample R² will be much higher (0.3–0.6) due to overfitting.`
},

