{id:'mc-logistic-why-not', type:'mc', channel:'logistic', ch_label:'Logistic Regression',
 text:'Why does this repo deliberately avoid logistic regression for predicting <code>fwd_ret_1d</code>?',
 opts:[
   {t:'The cross-sectional ranking step needs predicted magnitudes, not just direction. Binarizing the target (up/down) destroys the magnitude information that makes top-N selection work.',c:true,
    w:'Correct. The strategy sorts tickers by predicted return magnitude and picks the top N. If you classify up/down, all "up" predictions look the same — you can\'t rank them. Regression preserves the ordering.'},
   {t:'Logistic regression requires the target to be bounded in [0, 1], but returns can exceed ±100%.',c:false,
    w:'Wrong — logistic regression accepts binary labels {0, 1}, and the target is the binarized direction, not the return value itself. The issue is information loss, not a range constraint.'},
   {t:'Logistic regression has no closed-form solution, making it too slow to retrain monthly in walkforward_sim.py.',c:false,
    w:'Wrong — iterative logistic regression (LBFGS) is very fast in sklearn; speed is not the issue. The reason is the information loss from binarizing the continuous target.'},
   {t:'Logistic regression cannot handle continuous features like vol_10 and rsi_14 without binning them first.',c:false,
    w:'Wrong — logistic regression applies a linear combination of features (continuous or not) to a sigmoid; no binning is required.'},
 ]},

{id:'wr-log-loss', type:'wr', channel:'logistic', ch_label:'Logistic Regression',
 text:'Write the <strong>log-loss formula</strong> for binary classification. Why does this loss function produce calibrated probability estimates? Compare it to squared error.',
 concepts:[
   {label:'Formula: −[y·ln(p̂) + (1−y)·ln(1−p̂)]', terms:['log','ln','cross-entropy','y·ln','(1-y)','log loss','binary cross']},
   {label:'Unbounded penalty for confident wrongness', terms:['confident','wrong','infinite','unbounded','penali','0 log 0']},
   {label:'Gradient pushes p̂ toward calibrated frequency', terms:['calibrat','frequency','pushes','gradient','probability','real frequency']},
   {label:'Squared error does not calibrate probabilities', terms:['mse','squared','mean square','different','calibrat','bounded']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
<strong>Log-loss:</strong> ℓ = −[y·ln(p̂) + (1−y)·ln(1−p̂)] for y ∈ {0,1} and p̂ ∈ (0,1).
<br><br>
<strong>Why it calibrates:</strong> The loss is −ln(p̂) when y=1. As p̂ → 0 (confident wrong prediction), the loss → ∞. This asymmetric, unbounded penalty forces the model to never commit too confidently to wrong predictions. The global minimum occurs exactly when p̂ = P(y=1 | x) — the true conditional probability. Minimizing expected log-loss over the data distribution gives a calibrated model.
<br><br>
<strong>vs Squared error:</strong> MSE is bounded (max loss = 1 when y=1, p̂=0). A model can achieve low MSE by predicting 0.5 everywhere — not calibrated. Log-loss punishes overconfident wrong predictions far more severely, which forces the optimizer to estimate true frequencies.`
},

{id:'wr-sigmoid', type:'wr', channel:'logistic', ch_label:'Logistic Regression',
 text:'Write the <strong>sigmoid function</strong> formula. What are its outputs at z=0, z=+2, z=−2? Explain why it is the natural link function for converting a linear score to a probability.',
 concepts:[
   {label:'σ(z) = 1/(1+e⁻ᶻ)', terms:['1/(1+e','1 / (1+e','sigma','sigmoid','formula']},
   {label:'σ(0) = 0.5', terms:['0.5','0.50','half','z=0']},
   {label:'σ(2) ≈ 0.88, σ(−2) ≈ 0.12', terms:['0.88','0.12','88%','12%','z=2','z=-2','pm 2']},
   {label:'Maps ℝ → (0,1), interpreting score as probability', terms:['maps','real','(0,1)','probability','bounded','0 to 1','output']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
<strong>σ(z) = 1 / (1 + e⁻ᶻ)</strong>
<ul style="margin:.4rem 0 .4rem 1.2rem;line-height:1.8">
  <li>σ(0) = 0.5 — at zero score, model is maximally uncertain</li>
  <li>σ(+2) ≈ 0.88 — a strong positive score maps to 88% probability</li>
  <li>σ(−2) ≈ 0.12 — a strong negative score maps to 12% probability</li>
</ul>
<strong>Why it's natural:</strong> A linear model Xβ produces scores in (−∞, +∞). We need to compress that to a probability in (0, 1). The sigmoid is the natural choice because:
<ol style="margin:.4rem 0 .4rem 1.2rem;line-height:1.8">
  <li>It maps ℝ continuously to (0, 1) — never exactly 0 or 1, which keeps log-loss finite.</li>
  <li>It is monotonically increasing, preserving the ordering of scores.</li>
  <li>It arises from the logistic distribution; P(y=1|x) = σ(xᵀβ) is the maximum-likelihood model under the logistic family.</li>
</ol>`
},

