{id:'mc-strict-lt', type:'mc', channel:'wf', ch_label:'Walk-Forward & Eval',
 text:`In <code>walkforward_sim.py:210</code>, the rolling-retrain uses:
<pre class="snip">sub = f[f.index <span class="hi">&lt;</span> dt]   <span class="cm"># strict less-than</span></pre>
What would happen if this were changed to <code>f[f.index &lt;= dt]</code> (less-than-or-equal)?`,
 opts:[
   {t:'The model would train on day dt\'s features and label during the prediction step for day dt, introducing look-ahead bias.',c:true,
    w:'Correct. The label for row dt is fwd_ret_1d = next-day return, computed as ret.shift(-1). Including dt in training means the model saw dt\'s features paired with dt+1\'s return — future information — during the very prediction it makes for dt.'},
   {t:'The simulation would crash with a KeyError because dt does not exist in the feature index after the retrain.',c:false,
    w:'Wrong — the predict step (f.loc[dt]) is separate from the training step. Including dt in training is silent and runs without error; it\'s a subtle logical bug, not a runtime error.'},
   {t:'Training set grows by exactly one row, which is negligible for any realistic dataset of 250+ rows.',c:false,
    w:'Wrong — the problem is not the size of the training set but *which* one row is added. That row has a label derived from the next day\'s price — the exact return the model is about to predict. Even one leaked row creates systematic upward bias.'},
   {t:'The monthly retrain schedule would trigger one day earlier each month, causing a timing mismatch with the test evaluation.',c:false,
