{id:'wr-lookahead-two-layers', type:'wr', channel:'wf', ch_label:'Walk-Forward & Eval',
 text:'Describe the <strong>two layers of look-ahead bias</strong> that can corrupt an ML trading backtest. For each, give the specific form it takes in the context of this repo.',
 concepts:[
   {label:'Layer 1: feature/target leakage (shift direction)', terms:['shift','feature','target','fwd_ret','shift(-1)','shift(0)','label']},
   {label:'Layer 2: model training on test data', terms:['train','test','same data','in-sample','seen','future','training set includes']},
   {label:'Repo example of layer 1', terms:['paper_trading','build_features','shift(-1)','shift(0)']},
   {label:'Repo example of layer 2', terms:['f[f.index < dt]','<= dt','walkforward','retrain','seen future','monthly']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
<strong>Layer 1 — Feature/target leakage:</strong> The label for day t is <code>fwd_ret_1d = ret.shift(-1)</code> — tomorrow's return. If you accidentally used <code>shift(0)</code> (today's return), the model would learn to predict the present using features from the present — a triviality that scores perfectly in backtests but is unavailable in real trading (you don't know today's return before the day ends).
<br><br>
<strong>Layer 2 — Training leakage:</strong> If you train a model on all data 2016–2024 and then "backtest" it on the same 2016–2024 period, the model saw every test row during training. In-sample performance is inflated by memorization of noise patterns. In <code>walkforward_sim.py</code>, the guard is <code>f[f.index &lt; dt]</code> — strictly past data only — preventing this on every prediction date.`
},

{id:'wr-embargo', type:'wr', channel:'wf', ch_label:'Walk-Forward & Eval',
 text:'Explain the <strong>embargo gap</strong> in <code>walkforward_splits()</code>. Why is it needed when the label is <code>fwd_ret_1d = ret.shift(-1)</code>? How large should the embargo be in practice?',
 concepts:[
   {label:'fwd_ret_1d at row t references row t+1\'s price', terms:['shift(-1)','next day','t+1','future price','row t+1','tomorrow']},
   {label:'Last training row\'s label derived from first test-adjacent row', terms:['last training','first test','adjacent','label','contaminate','border']},
   {label:'Embargo of ≥1 day breaks that contamination', terms:['embargo','1 day','gap','break','protect','≥1','at least']},
   {label:'Longer embargo if rolling features overlap (MA, vol)', terms:['rolling','feature','ma','vol','overlap','window','longer','10 day','5 day']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
<strong>Why embargo is needed:</strong> The target at training row T is <code>fwd_ret_1d[T] = ret[T+1]</code> — it references day T+1's price. If the first test row is day T+1, the training set contains T+1's price embedded in the label of row T. Without embargo, the model has seen day T+1 during training.
<br><br>
<strong>Minimum embargo = 1 day</strong> breaks this: with embargo=1, the test window starts at T+2, so the training/label contamination is not present in any test row.
<br><br>
<strong>Longer embargo for rolling features:</strong> Features like <code>vol_10</code>, <code>ma_10</code>, and <code>rsi_14</code> use 10–14 day lookback windows. Rows immediately after the training boundary share overlapping feature windows with training rows — the features are correlated. A conservative approach is embargo ≥ max feature window (14 days for RSI), though in practice 1–5 days is common.`
},

