{id:'wr-capm-full', type:'wr', channel:'capm', ch_label:'CAPM as ML',
 text:`Your walk-forward backtest produces strategy equity. Describe how you would use OLS (via <code>pairs.py:_ols()</code>) to compute <strong>Jensen's alpha</strong> and <strong>beta</strong> for this strategy vs SPY. Include the regression specification and what output you care about.`,
 concepts:[
   {label:'y = strategy daily returns; x = SPY daily returns', terms:['strategy','spy','market','y = ','x = ','regress','returns']},
   {label:'OLS with intercept: X = [1s, market_ret]', terms:['intercept','ones','constant','column','np.column_stack','x.column','matrix']},
   {label:'Beta = slope coefficient (β̂[1])', terms:['slope','beta','β̂[1]','market exposure','coefficient[1]']},
   {label:'Alpha = intercept (β̂[0]) in annualized terms', terms:['intercept','alpha','β̂[0]','annualiz','× 252']},
   {label:'t-statistic to test if alpha is real', terms:['t-stat','t-statistic','significant','real','noise','se','standard error']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
<strong>Setup:</strong> Get daily simple returns from the strategy equity series (<code>equity.pct_change().dropna()</code>) and SPY returns over the same period.
<br><br>
<strong>OLS specification:</strong>
<pre style="background:#1e2e21;border-radius:.35rem;padding:.7rem;font-family:monospace;font-size:.82rem;margin:.5rem 0">
r_strategy = α + β·r_SPY + ε
X = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(r_spy)), r_spy])
beta, se = _ols(r_strategy, X)
alpha_daily, beta_market = beta[0], beta[1]
</pre>
<strong>Outputs:</strong>
<ul style="margin:.4rem 0 .4rem 1.2rem;line-height:1.8">
  <li><strong>β = beta[1]</strong>: market sensitivity. β ≈ 0 = market-neutral; β = 1 = full market exposure.</li>
  <li><strong>α = beta[0]</strong>: daily Jensen's alpha. Annualize: <code>alpha_annual = alpha_daily × 252</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>t-statistic</strong> = beta[0] / se[0]: is the alpha statistically distinguishable from zero? |t| > 2 is conventional significance. If t is small, the alpha may be noise.</li>
</ul>
<strong>Note:</strong> The lesson 0028 self-assignment proposes adding <code>alpha_beta()</code> to <code>analyze_equity.py</code> — this is the implementation.`
},

{id:'wr-capm-alpha-interpret', type:'wr', channel:'capm', ch_label:'CAPM as ML',
 text:'A strategy returns 18% annually. SPY returns 12%. Beta = 0.8. <br>(a) Compute Jensen\'s alpha. <br>(b) If a second strategy has the same 18% return but beta = 1.5, what is its alpha? <br>(c) Which strategy is more valuable to an investor who can already access SPY?',
 concepts:[
   {label:'Alpha = r_s − β × r_m', terms:['alpha','= r_s','− β','minus beta','subtract','formula','capm']},
   {label:'Strategy A alpha: 18% − 0.8×12% = 8.4%', terms:['8.4','8.4%','0.8 × 12','8.4 percent','strategy a']},
   {label:'Strategy B alpha: 18% − 1.5×12% = 0%', terms:['0%','zero','0 alpha','1.5 × 12','18%','strategy b']},
   {label:'Strategy A is more valuable (positive alpha vs zero alpha)', terms:['strategy a','more valuable','8.4','positive alpha','a is better','a wins']},
 ],
 answer:`<h4>Model Answer</h4>
<strong>(a) Strategy A alpha:</strong> α_A = 18% − 0.8 × 12% = 18% − 9.6% = <strong>8.4%</strong>
<br><strong>(b) Strategy B alpha:</strong> α_B = 18% − 1.5 × 12% = 18% − 18% = <strong>0%</strong>
<br><br>
<strong>(c) Strategy A is more valuable.</strong>
<br><br>
Strategy B earns 18% with β=1.5, but an investor who already holds SPY could get the same 18% by just putting 1.5× leverage into SPY (and SPY is free). Strategy B adds no value that can't be replicated cheaply.
<br><br>
Strategy A earns 8.4% alpha — a return that comes from genuine skill and cannot be replicated by any linear combination of SPY exposure. This is what investors pay hedge fund fees for.
<br><br>
This is the core intuition: beta is free (buy index funds); alpha is scarce (pay for it).`
},

